---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import HomeBiasLab from "../../components/HomeBiasLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Home Bias: How Much of the World Do You Own?"
  description="A globally diversified investor holds each country in proportion to its size. Almost nobody does. Pick your home market and see how small a slice of the world it really is — and what tilting toward home does to your diversification."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>How much of the world do you own?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Own the whole market, and you own it in proportion to each country's size —
      no bets on which nation wins next. Yet almost everyone piles into their home
      market far beyond its weight in the world. Pick where you invest from and
      see how big that tilt really is, and what it costs in diversification.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <HomeBiasLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The case for owning the world</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No country is the whole market.</strong> Even the US, the
          largest, is under two-thirds of global stock value; most home markets are
          a few percent or less. Holding only your home market means skipping most
          of the world's companies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Leadership rotates.</strong> The US led the 2010s; international
          stocks led the 2000s; Japan was a third of the world before a
          decades-long slump. Each era's winner felt obvious only in hindsight, and
          market-cap weighting quietly owns whoever is winning without having to
          guess.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Some home tilt is reasonable.</strong> You spend in your home
          currency, and home stocks can be cheaper to hold and more tax-friendly.
          A modest tilt is defensible; a large one is a concentrated bet on a single
          country's next few decades.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is the humility of indexing taken global: rather than guess the next
        winning country, own them all in proportion and rebalance as the map
        changes. Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="home-bias" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/home-bias" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
